## Prerequisites<a id="prerequisites"></a>

- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), or [nox](https://nox.thea.codes/) with
  [antsibull-nox](https://docs.ansible.com/projects/antsibull-nox/) in one
  Python environment.
- [Podman](https://podman.io/) for the container-backed `ansible-test` sessions.
- An authorized lab for each
  [Molecule scenario](./extensions/molecule/README.md).

## Checks<a id="checks"></a>

Run the checks from the collection's Git root. With nox installed, `nox`
replaces `uv run noxfile.py`.

```bash
uv run noxfile.py                 # fix, then run default checks
CI=true uv run noxfile.py         # check only
uv run noxfile.py -l              # list sessions
uv run noxfile.py -s lint         # Python, plugin YAML and typing
uv run noxfile.py -s helper-lint  # Python helpers
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-sanity
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-units
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-integration
uv run noxfile.py -s molecule
```

Among the default sessions:

- `runtime-check` installs the built collection in isolation and keeps only
  what ships: `meta/`, `plugins/`, `roles/` and `playbooks/`. It checks Python
  imports, module documentation and the syntax of any roles, then runs the
  unit tests against that installation. To check the exact archive of a
  release:

  ```sh
  uv run noxfile.py -s runtime-check -- --artifact /path/to/collection.tar.gz
  ```

- `helper-lint` checks the Python outside `plugins/` with
  [ruff.toml](ruff.toml): `extensions/molecule/`, any role's `files/`,
  `tools/`, `scripts/` and `tests/` apart from `tests/unit/`,
  `tests/integration/` and `tests/output/`. Add it to CI configurations that
  list sessions explicitly.

Put the collection's own pytest suites below `tests/unit/` so ansible-test
runs them. Other Python below `tests/` is linted by `helper-lint` but is not
run as a test suite.

Session definitions: [noxfile.py](noxfile.py).
Built-in session settings: [antsibull-nox.toml](antsibull-nox.toml).
While editing, run the sessions of one ansible-core release, such as
`uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-units-2.21` (`-l` lists them), and the
default sessions before a commit.

Below a deep checkout, nox gives each session a short symbolic link as
`TMPDIR`, because Unix socket paths are limited in length. A test of code that
refuses symbolic links in a path resolves `TMPDIR` first.

## Unit-test clocks<a id="unit-test-clocks"></a>

Use the opt-in `fake_clock` fixture when a test's expected outcome depends
on polling or a short deadline. It keeps the real `foundata.utils`
`Deadline`, but replaces its module-local clock. Patch each polling module
before invoking the code under test:

```python
fake_clock.patch(_activation)
```

`fake_clock.sleep(seconds)` advances virtual time immediately.
`fake_clock.advance(seconds)` models work in a fake handler, and
`fake_clock.now` exposes elapsed time. Budget the deadline for these
deliberate advances, not for host speed. Tests that do not request the
fixture keep real time; pytest restores all patches after each test.

`patch(module)` replaces that module's `time` reference, not Python's
global time functions. Modules that import a clock function directly or
capture it in a default argument need explicit injection instead.
Use this fixture only with synchronous fake products, not real I/O,
background workers or event loops. See `test_activation.py` for complete
polling and timeout examples.

## Boundaries<a id="boundaries"></a>

The CLI fixture is a hook-based existing-only resource. Its opaque expected
identity and composite selector are product assertions. The shared lifecycle
owns sequencing, prediction and reconciliation. `_engine.py` remains available
for products with a qualified definition model; this variant ships no
definition-driven CLI example. `_definitions.FIXTURE` names the hook class used
by the frozen resource registry.

The following files are invariant within collections rendered from this variant:

- `plugins/module_utils/`: `_lifecycle.py`, `_engine.py` and `_activation.py`.
- `tests/unit/plugins/`: `conftest.py` (including `fake_clock`), and
  `contracts.py`, `test_contract.py`, `test_engine.py`, `test_activation.py` and
  `test_lifecycle.py` in `modules/`.
- `extensions/molecule/shared/`: everything.

Leave these files unchanged when adapting a product. Propose a change in
[foundata/ansible-skeletons](https://github.com/foundata/ansible-skeletons) when
an invariant needs to change.

`tests/unit/plugins/modules/contract_subject.py` is product-adapted. It
registers actual public entry points, one of two explicit modes, and a fake
adapter. An existing-only subject requires a writer; an observational subject
forbids one. Both require an information entry point. Incomplete registrations
fail during collection. Observational-only products import no fabricated mutable
exports. Every public module must be registered.

`plugins/module_utils/_binding.py` and `_compat.py` retain their shared
structure with product policy. The client, hook resource, definitions,
documentation fragment, fake program, fake adapter, other unit tests,
integration targets and scenario tasks are product-owned. The fixture setup
target is product-owned because the executable, its seeded state and its faults
describe this product.

`foundata.utils` owns bounded byte transport, the monotonic deadline and safe
error translation. The client supplies the command grammar, parser, locator and
version probe. Credential input stays on stdin. Native concurrency guarantees
and locator qualification remain the product's responsibility.

The REST inventory extractor is omitted. Add a product-specific extractor when a
versioned CLI reference supplies a useful inventory; keep its output under
`tools/inventory/`, excluded from collection archives.

## Releases<a id="releases"></a>

Each change users notice adds a fragment to `changelogs/fragments/`;
`00-fragment-template.txt` lists the sections. `release` turns the fragments
into `CHANGELOG.rst` and `changelogs/changelog.yaml`, so neither file is edited
by hand.

Releases require `releasing>=7.0.0` for the `release` command. Install the
development requirement in the maintainer's Python environment:

```sh
python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
```

`release build` runs the committed revision's Ansible sanity tests before
building the archive. Set `repository` in `.releasing.toml` before the first
release.

